WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.460
RYAN MATSUMOTO: Cloud Bigtable
is Google Cloud's NoSQL

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.980
database designed to
handle large analytical and

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.930
operational workloads.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.790
In previous episodes,
we've learned

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.880
about Bigtable's speed,
scale, and reliability,

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.130
as well as how it's being
used by companies to deliver

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.379
personalized experiences
to their customers.

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:18.570
In this episode, we're going
to learn more about how

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.435
Bigtable can be used for another
key use case, fraud detection.

00:00:21.435 --> 00:00:24.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:31.200
The rapid growth of online
retail in recent years

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.480
has created new opportunities
for businesses, but also

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.980
many challenges.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:38.070
One big challenge for
retailers is detecting fraud.

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.860
This could include credit
card fraud, account takeover,

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.600
and promotion abuse.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.460
If fraud isn't
prevented, this could

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.160
lead to chargeback fees,
lost inventory, and loss

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.110
of customer trust.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.520
Retailers need to
be able to tell

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.187
if they should charge
a credit card when

00:00:52.187 --> 00:00:54.030
a customer makes a purchase.

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.010
At the same time,
they need to make sure

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.690
that legitimate
payments go through

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.150
and that the process is smooth
and efficient for everyone.

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.570
This is where fraud detection
companies come into play,

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.390
helping retailers detect fraud
with the power of big data

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:08.200
and machine learning.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.530
Bigtable is great for fraud
detection use cases because

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.870
of its scalability and speed.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:14.730
One challenge that fraud
detection companies

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.490
face is the massive
scale of their data.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.400
Whenever a fraud detection
company brings on a new client,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.190
a new set of data and throughput
gets added to their system.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:26.610
Some of these clients might
have thousands of stores,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.650
billions of dollars
in revenue per year,

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.650
and millions of
transactions per day.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:33.960
Luckily, Bigtable
has the scalability

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.000
necessary to handle
large volumes of data.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.570
Bigtable databases can be scaled
with the click of a button

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.060
by adding or removing
nodes, which provide up

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.950
to 10,000 additional
queries per second,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.472
as well as additional
storage capacity.

00:01:46.472 --> 00:01:48.180
This means that fraud
detection companies

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.510
can store up to petabytes
of data in Bigtable.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.220
Fraud detection
companies also need

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.370
to be able to access
this data at low latency.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.530
When a client's customer
places an order,

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:00.120
the fraud detection
company needs

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.890
to retrieve as much
data as possible

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:05.340
about that customer to
detect potential fraud.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.430
This data is then passed through
a machine learning model.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:10.020
But this whole
process also needs

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.772
to be done as
quickly as possible.

00:02:11.772 --> 00:02:13.230
You wouldn't want
customers to have

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.522
to wait several minutes for
the fraud detection process

00:02:15.522 --> 00:02:18.480
to complete before
they can buy items.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.610
Fortunately,
Bigtable's optimized

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.740
for handling large numbers
of reads and writes

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:24.930
per second at low
latency, on the order

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.660
of single-digit milliseconds.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.580
One way to go about
this is by storing data

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:32.790
for each customer in a single
row of a Bigtable table.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.610
This makes it extremely fast to
lookup data for that customer

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.250
when they attempt
to make a purchase.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.320
The row key can be
the customer ID,

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.650
and the columns can store data
about orders, transactions,

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.660
and payment methods.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.240
Versioning allows you to store
different versions of data

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:50.820
in a given row-column
intersection of a Bigtable

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:51.990
table.

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:53.758
For example, you
could use versioning

00:02:53.758 --> 00:02:55.800
to store data about each
of the different payment

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.070
methods a given customer
has used over time.

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:00.810
Garbage collection
makes it easy for you

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.860
to remove expired or obsolete
data from Bigtable tables.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.500
This can help you limit the size
of your Bigtable rows, which

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.960
can improve performance
and keep costs down.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:11.700
For fraud detection,
you might want

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.260
to set a garbage
collection policy

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:16.260
to only keep information about
a customer's last three payment

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.550
methods.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.010
Fraud detection
companies can also

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.510
take advantage of how easy it
is to integrate their Bigtable

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:24.900
database with BigQuery
for further analysis.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.790
Bigtable's low latency
and scalability

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.740
are great for using
it in the serving path

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.420
of your application.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.100
But if you want to perform
analysis on your entire data

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:36.720
set, we recommend using our
data warehouse, BigQuery.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.270
While it might make sense
to use Bigtable to look up

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.610
data about each
customer individually,

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:43.590
BigQuery could be better
for analyzing data

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.320
about multiple
customers at once.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.570
One way to go about this
is to send the same data

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.000
to both Bigtable and
BigQuery at the same time.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.720
This would allow data scientists
to run complex queries,

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.300
build models, and
investigate patterns

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.950
without affecting the underlying
performance of queries

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:03.330
to Bigtable for live
fraud detection.

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:06.480
As you can see, Bigtable's
scalability and speed

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.610
make it a great fit for
the unique challenges

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.200
of fraud detection.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.803
If you're curious to learn more,
take a look at the links below.

00:04:13.803 --> 00:04:14.970
Thanks for joining us today.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:17.160
And stay tuned for more
episodes about Bigtable.

